<BaseLayout title="Theory" description="Foundations for electronic-structure theory." current="theory">
  <h1>Theory</h1>
  <p class="lede">Electronic-structure theory rests on mathematics, physics, chemistry, and the theories developed specifically for interacting electrons.</p>
  <p class="secondary">The directory remains a map rather than a fixed curriculum. Thirty-nine subjects have individually reviewed pages; the route sections below connect them according to the learner's present knowledge gap rather than prescribing one universal order.</p>

  <section aria-labelledby="mathematical-foundations">
    <h2 id="mathematical-foundations">Mathematical Foundations</h2>
    <p>The mathematical language of states, operators, fields, approximation, and numerical solution.</p>
    <ul>
      <li id="linear-algebra"><a href={href('/theory/linear-algebra/')}>Linear Algebra</a> <span class="item-summary">— states, bases, operators, subspaces, and eigenvalue problems.</span></li>
      <li id="calculus-and-analysis"><a href={href('/theory/calculus-and-analysis/')}>Calculus and Analysis</a> <span class="item-summary">— change, integration, limits, and convergence.</span></li>
      <li id="differential-equations"><a href={href('/theory/differential-equations/')}>Differential Equations</a> <span class="item-summary">— continuous operators, initial and boundary conditions, spectral problems, and Green functions.</span></li>
      <li id="fourier-analysis"><a href={href('/theory/fourier-analysis/')}>Fourier Analysis</a> <span class="item-summary">— transforms, reciprocal components, convolution, sampling, DFTs, and aliasing.</span></li>
      <li id="functional-analysis-and-variational-methods"><a href={href('/theory/functional-analysis-and-variational-methods/')}>Functional Analysis and Variational Methods</a> <span class="item-summary">— Hilbert spaces, operator domains, weak formulations, and continuum variational principles.</span></li>
      <li id="numerical-analysis"><a href={href('/theory/numerical-analysis/')}>Numerical Analysis</a> <span class="item-summary">— error, conditioning, stability, discretization, and iterative solution.</span></li>
      <li id="probability-and-statistics"><a href={href('/theory/probability-and-statistics/')}>Probability and Statistics</a> <span class="item-summary">— random variables, sampling, correlations, inference, and uncertainty boundaries.</span></li>
      <li id="group-theory-and-symmetry"><a href={href('/theory/group-theory-and-symmetry/')}>Group Theory and Symmetry</a> <span class="item-summary">— representations, irreducible sectors, selection rules, little groups, and compatibility.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="physical-foundations">
    <h2 id="physical-foundations">Physical Foundations</h2>
    <p>The physical principles governing quantum systems, equilibrium, periodic matter, response, and interacting particles.</p>
    <ul>
      <li id="classical-mechanics"><a href={href('/theory/classical-mechanics/')}>Classical Mechanics</a> <span class="item-summary">— generalized coordinates, variational dynamics, normal modes, forces, and classical nuclei.</span></li>
      <li id="electromagnetism"><a href={href('/theory/electromagnetism/')}>Electromagnetism</a> <span class="item-summary">— Coulomb fields, Poisson equations, multipoles, polarization, dielectric response, and boundaries.</span></li>
      <li id="quantum-mechanics"><a href={href('/theory/quantum-mechanics/')}>Quantum Mechanics</a> <span class="item-summary">— states, operators, dynamics, spin, and identical fermions.</span></li>
      <li id="thermodynamics"><a href={href('/theory/thermodynamics/')}>Thermodynamics</a> <span class="item-summary">— state functions, thermodynamic potentials, equilibrium, chemical potentials, and phase stability.</span></li>
      <li id="statistical-mechanics"><a href={href('/theory/statistical-mechanics/')}>Statistical Mechanics</a> <span class="item-summary">— ensembles, Fermi occupations, entropy, finite-temperature free energies, and fluctuations.</span></li>
      <li id="atomic-and-molecular-physics"><a href={href('/theory/atomic-and-molecular-physics/')}>Atomic and Molecular Physics</a> <span class="item-summary">— bound states, angular momenta, fields, spectra, and selection rules.</span></li>
      <li id="solid-state-physics"><a href={href('/theory/solid-state-physics/')}>Solid-State Physics</a> <span class="item-summary">— periodicity, Bloch states, bands, filling, and collective degrees of freedom.</span></li>
      <li id="crystallography"><a href={href('/theory/crystallography/')}>Crystallography</a> <span class="item-summary">— lattices, cells, reciprocal space, diffraction, space groups, and Wyckoff positions.</span></li>
      <li id="many-body-physics"><a href={href('/theory/many-body-physics/')}>Many-Body Physics</a> <span class="item-summary">— second quantization, Green functions, quasiparticles, collective modes, and broken symmetry.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="chemical-foundations">
    <h2 id="chemical-foundations">Chemical Foundations</h2>
    <p>The concepts used to interpret atoms, bonds, molecules, solids, surfaces, and composition-dependent behavior.</p>
    <ul>
      <li id="general-chemistry"><a href={href('/theory/general-chemistry/')}>General Chemistry</a> <span class="item-summary">— atoms, composition, periodic trends, bonding, redox, equilibrium, and kinetics vocabulary.</span></li>
      <li id="physical-chemistry"><a href={href('/theory/physical-chemistry/')}>Physical Chemistry</a> <span class="item-summary">— thermodynamic, kinetic, quantum, and spectroscopic model layers for chemical questions.</span></li>
      <li id="quantum-chemistry"><a href={href('/theory/quantum-chemistry/')}>Quantum Chemistry</a> <span class="item-summary">— molecular Hamiltonians, Born–Oppenheimer separation, orbitals, antisymmetry, and correlation.</span></li>
      <li id="chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure"><a href={href('/theory/chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure/')}>Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure</a> <span class="item-summary">— orbital, density, symmetry, energy, and formal chemical interpretations.</span></li>
      <li id="inorganic-chemistry"><a href={href('/theory/inorganic-chemistry/')}>Inorganic Chemistry</a> <span class="item-summary">— oxidation states, coordination, ligand fields, d/f configurations, spin states, and distortions.</span></li>
      <li id="solid-state-chemistry"><a href={href('/theory/solid-state-chemistry/')}>Solid-State Chemistry</a> <span class="item-summary">— composition, structure types, defects, nonstoichiometry, doping, and phase relations.</span></li>
      <li id="surface-and-interface-chemistry"><a href={href('/theory/surface-and-interface-chemistry/')}>Surface and Interface Chemistry</a> <span class="item-summary">— surface energy, adsorption, work functions, reconstruction, alignment, and interface dipoles.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="electronic-structure-theory">
    <h2 id="electronic-structure-theory">Electronic Structure Theory</h2>
    <p>Focused theories and computational representations used to formulate and solve electronic many-electron problems.</p>
    <ul>
      <li id="many-electron-problem"><a href={href('/theory/many-electron-problem/')}>The Many-Electron Problem</a> <span class="item-summary">— interacting Hamiltonians, antisymmetry, state-space growth, observables, and correlation.</span></li>
      <li id="hartree-and-hartree-fock-methods"><a href={href('/theory/hartree-and-hartree-fock-theory/')}>Hartree and Hartree–Fock Theory</a> <span class="item-summary">— product states, determinants, mean fields, exchange, and single-determinant limits.</span></li>
      <li id="density-functional-theory"><a href={href('/theory/density-functional-theory-foundations/')}>Density Functional Theory: Foundations</a> <span class="item-summary">— ground-state density, universal functionals, constrained search, and variational scope.</span></li>
      <li id="kohn-sham-theory"><a href={href('/theory/kohn-sham-density-functional-theory/')}>Kohn–Sham Density Functional Theory</a> <span class="item-summary">— the auxiliary noninteracting system, effective potential, orbitals, and density reconstruction.</span></li>
      <li id="exchange-correlation-theory"><a href={href('/theory/exchange-correlation-functionals-and-approximations/')}>Exchange–Correlation Functionals and Approximations</a> <span class="item-summary">— exact role, approximation families, constraints, implementation, and failure modes.</span></li>
      <li id="self-consistent-field-methods"><a href={href('/theory/self-consistent-field-methods/')}>Self-Consistent Field Methods</a> <span class="item-summary">— nonlinear fixed points, residuals, mixing, occupations, and metastability.</span></li>
      <li id="basis-sets-and-numerical-representations"><a href={href('/theory/discretization-and-basis-representations/')}>Discretization and Basis Representations</a> <span class="item-summary">— finite spaces, grids, boundary conditions, completeness, and observable-specific convergence.</span></li>
      <li id="plane-wave-and-real-space-methods"><a href={href('/theory/plane-wave-and-real-space-methods/')}>Plane-Wave and Real-Space Methods</a> <span class="item-summary">— spectral bases, real-space families, FFT grids, boundary conditions, and representation errors.</span></li>
      <li id="localized-orbital-methods"><a href={href('/theory/localized-orbital-methods/')}>Localized-Orbital Methods</a> <span class="item-summary">— atom-centred bases, nonorthogonality, Bloch sums, tight binding, and basis convergence.</span></li>
      <li id="pseudopotentials-and-paw"><a href={href('/theory/pseudopotentials-paw-and-core-valence-treatments/')}>Pseudopotentials, PAW, and Core–Valence Treatments</a> <span class="item-summary">— frozen cores, projectors, augmentation, reconstruction, transferability, and validation.</span></li>
      <li id="brillouin-zone-sampling"><a href={href('/theory/brillouin-zone-sampling/')}>Brillouin-Zone Sampling</a> <span class="item-summary">— k-space integration, symmetry weights, occupations, smearing, and observable convergence.</span></li>
      <li id="relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism"><a href={href('/theory/relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism/')}>Relativistic Electronic Structure, Spin, and Magnetism</a> <span class="item-summary">— scalar relativity, spinors, SOC, noncollinearity, magnetic symmetry, and anisotropy.</span></li>
      <li id="linear-response-and-excited-states"><a href={href('/theory/linear-response-and-excited-states/')}>Linear Response and Excited States</a> <span class="item-summary">— response kernels, DFPT, TDDFT, spectra, and neutral excitations.</span></li>
      <li id="many-body-perturbation-theory-and-quasiparticles"><a href={href('/theory/many-body-perturbation-theory-and-quasiparticles/')}>Many-Body Perturbation Theory and Quasiparticles</a> <span class="item-summary">— screened self-energy, GW quasiparticles, BSE excitons, and charged/neutral excitation boundaries.</span></li>
      <li id="berry-phases-and-electronic-topology"><a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry Phases and Electronic Topology</a> <span class="item-summary">— gauge structure, polarization, curvature, Chern numbers, Wilson loops, and topology evidence.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="reviewed-study-routes">
    <h2 id="reviewed-study-routes">Reviewed Study Routes</h2>
    <p>Course selection is not restricted to MIT OpenCourseWare or major publishers. Chinese university courses, independent creators, long-form explanations, and open-source notebooks are included when their authorship, structure, technical scope, access, and limitations can be checked. Social-platform recommendations are used to discover repeatedly useful resources; popularity is never sufficient for acceptance.</p>

    <h3>Mathematical intuition and formalism</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.3blue1brown.com/topics/linear-algebra">3Blue1Brown’s <cite>Linear Algebra</cite> sequence</a> is a visual pre-course for span, basis, linear transformations, determinants, eigenvectors, and change of coordinates. It is frequently paired with formal courses in community study discussions because it repairs geometric intuition quickly, but it does not replace definitions, proofs, computation, or numerical stability.</p>
    <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06-linear-algebra-spring-2010/">MIT OpenCourseWare 18.06, <cite>Linear Algebra</cite>, taught by Gilbert Strang</a> supplies the corresponding full course through elimination, subspaces, orthogonality, eigenvalues, positive-definite matrices, and singular-value decomposition. It is a strong first formal route before <a href={href('/theory/quantum-mechanics/')}>Quantum Mechanics</a> and <a href={href('/theory/numerical-analysis/')}>Numerical Analysis</a>, while generalized sparse eigensolvers remain a later topic.</p>

    <h3>Quantum mechanics</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1yh4y1A7ja/"><cite lang="zh-CN">B站物院の量子力学</cite></a> is a compact self-produced Chinese sequence covering wavefunctions, Schrödinger equations, model potentials, operators, Hilbert-space notation, representations, hydrogen, spin, angular momentum, and identical particles. It is useful for a first pass or structured review, but its short chapters should be paired with a textbook and sustained problem solving.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.zhihu.com/tardis/zm/art/169805550"><cite lang="zh-CN">从线性代数到量子力学</cite> by PeiLingX</a> uses spin experiments, basis changes, probability distributions, Fourier transforms, and uncertainty to bridge <a href={href('/theory/linear-algebra/')}>Linear Algebra</a> and <a href={href('/theory/quantum-mechanics/')}>Quantum Mechanics</a>. It is a conceptual bridge rather than a complete course.</p>

    <h3>Electromagnetism</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1ki4y1A7Mh/"><cite lang="zh-CN">大学物理《电磁学》</cite>, University of Science and Technology of China School of the Gifted Young</a> is a 116-lecture official course recording covering electrostatics, dielectric media, boundary-value methods, magnetism, induction, Maxwell equations, and waves. It provides a complete Chinese undergraduate route before the Atlas narrows the subject to periodic electrostatics and response.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Kk4y1e7He/"><cite lang="zh-CN">普通物理学—电磁学</cite> by 大T-Peachsea</a> is a completed handwritten independent sequence with detailed derivations and examples. It is useful for calculation-following support; the creator's own undergraduate framing means it should supplement, not replace, established textbooks.</p>

    <h3>Thermodynamics and statistical mechanics</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Zt4y1E7GV/"><cite lang="zh-CN">热力学与统计物理</cite>, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, taught by Zou Yuanchuan</a> is an original Chinese course recording with a visible sequence from thermodynamic laws and homogeneous media through ensembles, ideal Fermi and Bose gases, phase transitions, and introductory irreversible processes. It is a coherent undergraduate route before the Atlas separates physical temperature, electronic occupations, phonon free energies, numerical smearing, and finite sampling.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/">David Tong’s <cite>Statistical Physics</cite> notes</a> provide an author-maintained text route through ensembles, classical and quantum gases, thermodynamics, phase transitions, fluctuations, and Landau theory. They are suited to readers who prefer compact derivations and consistent notation; they are not a finite-temperature DFT or molecular-dynamics manual.</p>

    <h3>Chemistry-first electronic structure</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/av752549261"><cite lang="zh-CN">量子化学入门</cite> by Druid小德</a> is a completed self-produced Chinese sequence that moves from quantum postulates and basis representations through Born–Oppenheimer separation, Hartree–Fock, configuration interaction, active spaces, coupled cluster, DFT, relativistic effects, vibrations, and transitions. It is useful for chemistry-first learners who need to see how molecular language connects to electronic-structure methods, but the creator explicitly presents it as an independent course with possible errors; standard texts and the individual Atlas pages remain the checking layer.</p>

    <h3>Solid-state physics</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.icourse163.org/course/NUDT-1206139804"><cite lang="zh-CN">简明固体物理</cite>, National University of Defense Technology</a> covers crystal structure, lattice vibrations, free electrons, and band theory in a lower-barrier course of roughly ten video hours. It is a compact entry route rather than a full advanced treatment of magnetism, topology, or many-body response.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Hs4y1n7T8/"><cite lang="zh-CN">B站物院の固体物理</cite></a> is a concise eight-chapter independent series with paired exercises on crystal structure, reciprocal lattices, bonding, lattice vibrations, phonon thermodynamics, the free-electron gas, bands, and semiconductors. Its compressed form makes it better for review than for proof-level study.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.davidtong.org/teaching/solid-state-physics/">David Tong’s <cite>Solid State Physics</cite> notes</a> form a compact continuation through band structure, Fermi surfaces, semiclassical electron dynamics, phonons, tight binding, nearly-free electrons, Wannier functions, and magnetic fields. They are especially useful after introductory quantum mechanics; they do not replace crystallography, interacting-electron theory, or material-specific validation.</p>

    <h3>Density-functional and Kohn–Sham theory</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1spj4zgEv3/"><cite lang="zh-CN">密度泛函理论（2025年5月）</cite> by 孙老师讲物理</a> is a six-part Chinese sequence connecting Thomas–Fermi, Thomas–Fermi–Dirac, Hohenberg–Kohn variation, Kohn–Sham equations, and the local-density approximation. It offers a coherent narrative bridge, while the Atlas continues to separate DFT foundations, the Kohn–Sham construction, XC approximation, and SCF solution.</p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/tjz21/DFT_PIB_Code"><code>tjz21/DFT_PIB_Code</code></a> provides MIT-licensed Jupyter and Colab notebooks that expose the Kohn–Sham potential, LDA/PBE choices, density updates, eigenvalues, and SCF iteration through pedagogical model systems. It is an executable introduction, not evidence for production-material accuracy.</p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/JuliaMolSim/DFTK.jl"><code>JuliaMolSim/DFTK.jl</code></a> is an actively maintained MIT-licensed plane-wave DFT toolkit built for numerical and algorithmic transparency. Its tutorials connect the physical model to cutoffs, k-point sampling, and SCF solution, but require Julia and substantially more numerical maturity.</p>

    <h3>Integrated theoretical-physics notes</h3>
    <p><a href="https://www.davidtong.org/teaching/">David Tong’s author-maintained teaching archive</a> provides connected notes for classical mechanics, electromagnetism, quantum mechanics, statistical physics, solid-state physics, kinetic theory, and the quantum Hall effect. It is useful when consistent notation across several prerequisite subjects matters more than following one institution's course sequence. The levels vary from early undergraduate to graduate, and the archive is not an electronic-structure curriculum.</p>

    <h3>Variational and real-space numerics</h3>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/JSchoeberl/iFEM"><code>JSchoeberl/iFEM</code></a> is an interactive Jupyter-book course on finite-element theory and computation. It is not an electronic-structure course, but it gives a rigorous experimental bridge from weak formulations and Sobolev spaces to real-space discretization for <a href={href('/theory/differential-equations/')}>Differential Equations</a>, <a href={href('/theory/functional-analysis-and-variational-methods/')}>Functional Analysis</a>, and numerical representation.</p>

    <p>Third-party university-course reuploads, textbook-download bundles, marketing-first training pages, exam-cram fragments, and repositories with unclear provenance or unresolved copyright boundaries are excluded. Play count, follower count, stars, and institutional branding are discovery signals only.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="learning-map">
    <h2 id="learning-map">Learning Map</h2>
    <p>The Learning Map connects detailed concepts through prerequisites, branches, and converging paths. Choose the route that repairs the present knowledge gap; do not complete every page before beginning electronic structure.</p>

    <h3>Physics-first route</h3>
    <p>Begin with <a href={href('/theory/linear-algebra/')}>Linear Algebra</a> and the parts of <a href={href('/theory/calculus-and-analysis/')}>Calculus and Analysis</a> needed for wavefunctions and operators. Continue through <a href={href('/theory/quantum-mechanics/')}>Quantum Mechanics</a>, <a href={href('/theory/statistical-mechanics/')}>Statistical Mechanics</a>, <a href={href('/theory/crystallography/')}>Crystallography</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/solid-state-physics/')}>Solid-State Physics</a>. Enter electronic-structure theory through <a href={href('/theory/many-electron-problem/')}>The Many-Electron Problem</a>, <a href={href('/theory/hartree-and-hartree-fock-theory/')}>Hartree–Fock</a>, <a href={href('/theory/density-functional-theory-foundations/')}>DFT Foundations</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/kohn-sham-density-functional-theory/')}>Kohn–Sham DFT</a>.</p>

    <h3>Chemistry-first route</h3>
    <p>Use <a href={href('/theory/general-chemistry/')}>General Chemistry</a> only to repair atomic, periodic, equilibrium, and bonding vocabulary, then move through <a href={href('/theory/physical-chemistry/')}>Physical Chemistry</a>, <a href={href('/theory/quantum-chemistry/')}>Quantum Chemistry</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure/')}>Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure</a>. Add <a href={href('/theory/inorganic-chemistry/')}>Inorganic Chemistry</a>, <a href={href('/theory/solid-state-chemistry/')}>Solid-State Chemistry</a>, or <a href={href('/theory/surface-and-interface-chemistry/')}>Surface and Interface Chemistry</a> according to the intended systems, then join the many-electron, Hartree–Fock, DFT, and Kohn–Sham sequence.</p>

    <h3>Numerics-first route</h3>
    <p>When the physical equations are understandable but calculations remain opaque, follow <a href={href('/theory/linear-algebra/')}>Linear Algebra</a>, <a href={href('/theory/differential-equations/')}>Differential Equations</a>, <a href={href('/theory/fourier-analysis/')}>Fourier Analysis</a>, <a href={href('/theory/functional-analysis-and-variational-methods/')}>Functional Analysis and Variational Methods</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/numerical-analysis/')}>Numerical Analysis</a>. Then compare <a href={href('/theory/discretization-and-basis-representations/')}>Discretization and Basis Representations</a>, <a href={href('/theory/plane-wave-and-real-space-methods/')}>Plane-Wave and Real-Space Methods</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/localized-orbital-methods/')}>Localized-Orbital Methods</a> before studying <a href={href('/theory/self-consistent-field-methods/')}>SCF Methods</a>, <a href={href('/theory/brillouin-zone-sampling/')}>Brillouin-Zone Sampling</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/pseudopotentials-paw-and-core-valence-treatments/')}>Core–Valence Treatments</a>.</p>

    <h3>Advanced response, correlation, and topology route</h3>
    <p>Start only after the ground-state Kohn–Sham construction, representation choices, and sampling are clear. Combine <a href={href('/theory/group-theory-and-symmetry/')}>Group Theory and Symmetry</a>, <a href={href('/theory/many-body-physics/')}>Many-Body Physics</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism/')}>Relativistic Electronic Structure, Spin, and Magnetism</a>. Continue to <a href={href('/theory/linear-response-and-excited-states/')}>Linear Response and Excited States</a>, <a href={href('/theory/many-body-perturbation-theory-and-quasiparticles/')}>Many-Body Perturbation Theory and Quasiparticles</a>, or <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry Phases and Electronic Topology</a> according to the observable. These branches are not interchangeable: ground-state derivatives, neutral excitations, charged quasiparticles, and topological invariants require different formalisms and evidence.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
